    laws: [],
    what: 'Livelihood, women’s empowerment and poverty alleviation through women’s self-help groups, one of DEHAT’s longest continuous engagements — running from 2003 to 2016 and growing from 50 groups in Chittaura block to a peak of 263 groups across Chittaura and Payagpur.',
    why: 'Self-help groups give women a financial identity a bank will recognise, which is the precondition for credit, and a standing meeting, which is the precondition for collective action on anything else.',
    how: 'Group formation, leader training and bank linkage. Groups saved a monthly premium of ₹20 and lent internally before seeking external credit. Leaders were trained in residential batches and taken on exposure visits to established groups — 89 leaders visited five villages of Chittaura on 29 January 2008 to learn record keeping and group management from women already doing it. DEHAT’s direct facilitation is last evidenced in 2007–08; the self-help groups it organised across Chittaura and Payagpur blocks were built to save, lend and manage their own accounts, and continued doing so on their own once direct facilitation ended.',
